-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/11aaf921-d352-4ee5-be80-75bd243fd6dd
-- title:
--   Connes–Feldman–Weiss §§1–3 — discrete measured equivalence relations, the measure m and module δ, type I and hyperfinite relations, finite subrelations, bounded sets
-- statement:
--   Definitions from Connes, Feldman and Weiss, *An amenable equivalence relation is generated by a single transformation*, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 1 (1981) 431–450, https://doi.org/10.1017/S014338570000136X, §§1–3 (pp. 433–435). Amenability of a relation (Definitions 5–6, pp. 436–437) is the published `Monod.IsAmenableRel`.
--
--   - `saturation R A` and `IsDiscreteMeasured μ R` are the setting of §1, p. 433: “Let $X$ be a standard Borel space and $R$ a Borel subset of $X \times X$ which is an equivalence relation. We say that $R$ is discrete if each $R$ equivalence class is countable. A measure $\mu$ on $X$ is said to be quasi-invariant for $R$ if, for every $\mu$-null Borel set $A \subset X$, the saturation of $A$, $R(A) = \{x \in X, \exists y \in A, (x, y) \in R\}$, is $\mu$-null.” `saturation R A` is $R(A)$, and `IsDiscreteMeasured μ R` says that $R$ is a Borel equivalence relation with countable classes for which $\mu$ is quasi-invariant: a “discrete measured equivalence relation”, the paper's name for this setting, which it never defines in a single sentence.
--   - `relMeasure μ R` and `module μ R` are the measure $m$ and the module $\delta$ of p. 434: “There is a Borel homomorphism $\delta$, $\delta((x, y) \cdot (y, z)) = \delta(x, y)\delta(y, z)$, $\forall x, y, z$, from $R$ to $]0, +\infty[$, called the module of $\mu$ such that, for every partial transformation $\phi \in R$, one has $d\mu(\phi(x))/d\mu(x) = \delta(\phi(x), x)$ a.e. on $\operatorname{Dom}\phi$. One endows $R$ with the measure $m = \int \nu^x \, d\mu(x)$, where $\nu^x$ is the counting measure on $R^x = \{(x, y), y \sim x\}$, or with the equivalent measure $m^{-1}$, $dm^{-1}(x, y) = \delta^{-1}(x, y)\, dm(x, y)$ which is the image of $m$ by $(x, y) \in R \mapsto (y, x) = (x, y)^{-1}$.” `relMeasure μ R` is $m$, defined as a bind of $\mu$ against these counting measures. `module μ R` is $\delta$, defined as the Radon–Nikodym derivative of $m$ against its image $m^{-1}$ under $(x, y) \mapsto (y, x)$, which is how the quoted sentence relates them; it is therefore a cocycle only almost everywhere, not the everywhere-defined Borel homomorphism of the quotation. That $m$ is a well-defined measure of the stated form and that $\delta$ satisfies $d\mu(\phi(x))/d\mu(x) = \delta(\phi(x), x)$ for partial transformations $\phi \subset R$ both rest on the setting's hypotheses (classically through the Lusin–Novikov theorem); outside them the two Lean definitions return Mathlib's default values. (“$\phi \in R$” in the quotation means $\phi \subset R$.)
--   - `unitSpace T` and `IsFiniteSubrelation R T` are §2, pp. 434–435: “By a finite subequivalence relation (f.s.r.), we mean a measurable subset $T$ of $R$ which is the graph of an equivalence relation with finite equivalence classes on the subset $T^{(0)} = T \cap R^{(0)} = \{x \in X, (x, x) \in T\}$ of $X$.” `unitSpace T` is $T^{(0)}$, and `IsFiniteSubrelation R T` says that $T \subseteq R$ is measurable and is the graph of an equivalence relation with finite classes on $T^{(0)}$.
--   - `IsBoundedSubset μ R K` is §3, p. 435: “Let $K$ be a measurable subset of $R$. We shall say that $K$ is bounded if $\operatorname{Sup}_x |K_x|$, $\operatorname{Sup}_y |K^y|$, $\operatorname{Sup}_K |\operatorname{Log} \delta|$ are all finite.” Here $K_x$ and $K^y$ are the sections of $K$. The last is written as $c \le \delta \le c^{-1}$ for some $c > 0$, $m$-almost everywhere on $K$: $\delta$ is defined only almost everywhere, and the two-sided bound avoids reading $\log$ of the values $0$ and $\infty$.
--   - `cutOff R N` is $R$ off a set $N$: the pairs of $R$ with both points outside $N$, together with the diagonal, so that each point of $N$ is a class of its own; it encodes “$R$ restricted to $X \setminus N$” as a relation on $X$.
--   - `IsTypeI μ R` is Definition 1(1), p. 434: “We say that the discrete measured equivalence relation $R$ is of type I (or smooth) when the quotient Borel space is, up to a null set, a standard space.” Read with its subject: there is a conull $R$-saturated Borel set $X_0$ such that $R$ restricted to $X_0$ is a discrete measured equivalence relation (`IsDiscreteMeasured μ (cutOff R X₀ᶜ)`) and the quotient of $X_0$ by $R$, with the quotient $\sigma$-algebra, is a standard Borel space.
--   - `IsHyperfinite μ R` is Definition 1(2), p. 434: “We say that the discrete measured equivalence relation $R$ is hyperfinite (or approximately smooth) when it is, up to a null set, a countable increasing union of type I equivalence relations.” “Up to a null set” is read as covering the subject as well: there is a $\mu$-null Borel set $N$ such that $R$ off $N$ is a discrete measured equivalence relation (`IsDiscreteMeasured μ (cutOff R N)`) and, for points outside $N$, $R$ is the union of an increasing sequence of Borel equivalence relations of type I. Where a statement assumes `IsDiscreteMeasured μ R`, the first clause is automatic; it matters where a statement concludes that a relation it builds is hyperfinite (Corollaries 12–14). There the null set cannot be dropped in general: a non-singular map of $\mathbb R$ with finite fibres that agrees with the identity almost everywhere can have a hyperfinite tail relation for which Lebesgue measure is not quasi-invariant ([`ConnesFeldmanWeiss.exists_isHyperfinite_not_isDiscreteMeasured_tailRel`](https://prove2.me/theorems/f372363e-612d-4141-8b54-5f0931fbf867)).
--   - Partial transformations are the published `Monod.PartialTransformation R` (Monod bundle), used by Lemma 3 and the p. 434 milestone. It matches p. 433: “By a partial transformation $\phi$ of $X$ we mean a pair of measurable subsets $\operatorname{Dom}\phi$, $\operatorname{Im}\phi \subset X$ and a measurable bijection $\phi$ from $\operatorname{Dom}\phi$ to $\operatorname{Im}\phi$. We identify $\phi$ with its graph, $\{(x, \phi(x)), x \in \operatorname{Dom}\phi\}$, and write $\phi \subset R$ if one has $(x, \phi(x)) \in R$ for all $x \in \operatorname{Dom}\phi$.” The structure has “$\phi \subset R$” built in, and also asks that the inverse be measurable, which in a standard Borel space is automatic (Lusin–Souslin).
--   - `IsNonsingular μ f`, for a map $f : X \to X$: $f$ is measurable, and a measurable set is null exactly when its preimage is. This is the usual meaning of a non-singular transformation, which the paper uses for the transformation of the goal and the maps of Corollaries 12–13 without defining it. The paper's only gloss of the word is on p. 434 and concerns a partial transformation $\phi \subset R$ (a Borel bijection between Borel subsets of $X$ whose graph lies in $R$): “If $\phi \subset R$ then the image by $\phi$ of a Borel $\mu$-null set is $\mu$-null, i.e. $\phi$ is non-singular.” That is a consequence of quasi-invariance for every such $\phi$, not a hypothesis: it is the milestone `ConnesFeldmanWeiss.measure_image_eq_zero_of_partialTransformation`, so the bundle defines no separate notion of a non-singular partial transformation. For a Borel automorphism $T$ of $X$, `IsNonsingular μ T` says exactly that images and preimages of null sets are null; for a map that is not injective it does not imply that images of null sets are null.
--   - `IsAmenableGroup P` is amenability of a locally compact group, used in Corollary 14 and not defined in the paper: $L^\infty(P)$ for a Haar measure carries a left invariant mean, a linear, positive functional on bounded measurable functions, equal to $1$ at the constant $1$, insensitive to Haar-null changes and invariant under left translation.
-- source:
--   Connes, A., Feldman, J., Weiss, B., An amenable equivalence relation is generated by a single transformation, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 1 (1981) 431–450, https://doi.org/10.1017/S014338570000136X, pp. 433–435, §§1–3 (discrete measured equivalence relations, m, δ, type I, hyperfinite, f.s.r., bounded subsets)

import Mathlib

/-!
# Connes–Feldman–Weiss (1981): discrete measured equivalence relations

Definitions from A. Connes, J. Feldman, B. Weiss, *An amenable equivalence relation is generated by
a single transformation*, Ergodic Theory Dynam. Systems 1 (1981) 431–450, §§1–3 (pp. 433–435).
Amenability of a relation (Definitions 5–6, pp. 436–437) is `Monod.IsAmenableRel`.
-/

open MeasureTheory

namespace ConnesFeldmanWeiss

variable {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X]

/-- The saturation `R(A) = {x | ∃ y ∈ A, (x, y) ∈ R}` of a set `A` (p. 433). -/
def saturation (R : Set (X × X)) (A : Set X) : Set X :=
  {x | ∃ y ∈ A, (x, y) ∈ R}

/-- `R` is a discrete measured equivalence relation for `μ` (§1, p. 433): a Borel equivalence
relation with countable classes for which `μ` is quasi-invariant (the saturation of a null Borel
set is null). -/
structure IsDiscreteMeasured (μ : Measure X) (R : Set (X × X)) : Prop where
  measurableSet : MeasurableSet R
  equivalence : Equivalence fun x y => (x, y) ∈ R
  countable_classes : ∀ x, {y | (x, y) ∈ R}.Countable
  quasiInvariant : ∀ A, MeasurableSet A → μ A = 0 → μ (saturation R A) = 0

/-- The measure `m = ∫ νˣ dμ(x)` on `R` (p. 434), `νˣ` the counting measure on
`Rˣ = {(x, y) | y ~ x}`. -/
noncomputable def relMeasure (μ : Measure X) (R : Set (X × X)) : Measure (X × X) :=
  μ.bind fun x => (Measure.count.restrict {y | (x, y) ∈ R}).map (Prod.mk x)

/-- The module `δ` of `μ` (p. 434): the density of `m` against its image `m⁻¹` under
`(x, y) ↦ (y, x)`, so that `dm⁻¹ = δ⁻¹ dm`. -/
noncomputable def module (μ : Measure X) (R : Set (X × X)) : X × X → ENNReal :=
  (relMeasure μ R).rnDeriv ((relMeasure μ R).map Prod.swap)

/-- The unit space `T⁽⁰⁾ = {x | (x, x) ∈ T}` of a subset `T` of `R` (p. 434). -/
def unitSpace (T : Set (X × X)) : Set X :=
  {x | (x, x) ∈ T}

/-- A finite subequivalence relation (f.s.r.) of `R` (§2, p. 434): a measurable subset `T` of `R`
which is the graph of an equivalence relation with finite classes on `T⁽⁰⁾`. -/
structure IsFiniteSubrelation (R T : Set (X × X)) : Prop where
  measurableSet : MeasurableSet T
  subset : T ⊆ R
  mem_unitSpace : ∀ p ∈ T, p.1 ∈ unitSpace T ∧ p.2 ∈ unitSpace T
  symm : ∀ x y, (x, y) ∈ T → (y, x) ∈ T
  trans : ∀ x y z, (x, y) ∈ T → (y, z) ∈ T → (x, z) ∈ T
  finite_classes : ∀ x, {y | (x, y) ∈ T}.Finite

/-- A bounded subset `K` of `R` (§3, p. 435): measurable, with `Supₓ |Kₓ|`, `Sup_y |K^y|` and
`Sup_K |log δ|` finite; here `K^y = {(y, x) ∈ K}`, `Kₓ = {(y, x) ∈ K}` for fixed `x`, and the last
condition reads `c ≤ δ ≤ c⁻¹` for some `c > 0`, `m`-almost everywhere on `K`. -/
structure IsBoundedSubset (μ : Measure X) (R K : Set (X × X)) : Prop where
  measurableSet : MeasurableSet K
  subset : K ⊆ R
  bdd_snd : ∃ n : ℕ, ∀ x, {y | (y, x) ∈ K}.encard ≤ n
  bdd_fst : ∃ n : ℕ, ∀ y, {x | (y, x) ∈ K}.encard ≤ n
  bdd_module : ∃ c : NNReal, 0 < c ∧ ∀ᵐ p ∂relMeasure μ R, p ∈ K →
    (c : ENNReal) ≤ module μ R p ∧ module μ R p ≤ (c : ENNReal)⁻¹

/-- `R` off `N`: the pairs of `R` with both points outside `N`, together with the diagonal, so
that every point of `N` is a class of its own. -/
def cutOff (R : Set (X × X)) (N : Set X) : Set (X × X) :=
  {p | (p ∈ R ∧ p.1 ∉ N ∧ p.2 ∉ N) ∨ p.1 = p.2}

/-- `R` is of type I, or smooth (Definition 1(1), p. 434): up to a null set, `R` is a discrete
measured equivalence relation whose quotient Borel space is a standard Borel space. Here: there is a
conull, `R`-saturated Borel set `X₀` such that `R` off the complement of `X₀` is discrete measured and
the quotient of `X₀` by `R`, with the quotient σ-algebra, is standard Borel. -/
def IsTypeI (μ : Measure X) (R : Set (X × X)) : Prop :=
  ∃ X₀ : Set X, MeasurableSet X₀ ∧ μ X₀ᶜ = 0 ∧ (∀ x y, (x, y) ∈ R → (x ∈ X₀ ↔ y ∈ X₀)) ∧
    IsDiscreteMeasured μ (cutOff R X₀ᶜ) ∧
    StandardBorelSpace (Quot fun a b : X₀ => ((a : X), (b : X)) ∈ R)

/-- `R` is hyperfinite, or approximately smooth (Definition 1(2), p. 434): up to a null set, `R` is
a discrete measured equivalence relation and a countable increasing union of Borel equivalence
relations of type I. Here: there is a null Borel set `N` such that `R` off `N` is discrete measured
and, for points outside `N`, `R` is the union of an increasing sequence of type I relations. -/
def IsHyperfinite (μ : Measure X) (R : Set (X × X)) : Prop :=
  ∃ N : Set X, MeasurableSet N ∧ μ N = 0 ∧ IsDiscreteMeasured μ (cutOff R N) ∧
    ∃ S : ℕ → Set (X × X), Monotone S ∧
      (∀ n, MeasurableSet (S n) ∧ Equivalence (fun x y => (x, y) ∈ S n) ∧ IsTypeI μ (S n)) ∧
      ∀ x y, x ∉ N → y ∉ N → ((x, y) ∈ R ↔ ∃ n, (x, y) ∈ S n)

/-- A map `f : X → X` is non-singular for `μ`: measurable, and a measurable set is null exactly
when its preimage is. -/
def IsNonsingular (μ : Measure X) (f : X → X) : Prop :=
  Measurable f ∧ ∀ A, MeasurableSet A → (μ (f ⁻¹' A) = 0 ↔ μ A = 0)

/-- A locally compact group `P` is amenable: `L^∞(P)` for a Haar measure carries a left invariant
mean, a functional on bounded measurable functions that is linear, positive, takes the value `1` at
the constant function `1`, ignores Haar-null modifications and is invariant under left
translation. -/
def IsAmenableGroup (P : Type*) [Group P] [TopologicalSpace P] [IsTopologicalGroup P]
    [LocallyCompactSpace P] [MeasurableSpace P] [BorelSpace P] : Prop :=
  ∃ M : (P → ℝ) → ℝ,
    (∀ f g : P → ℝ, Measurable f → Measurable g → (∃ C, ∀ x, |f x| ≤ C) → (∃ C, ∀ x, |g x| ≤ C) →
      M (f + g) = M f + M g ∧ (f =ᵐ[Measure.haar] g → M f = M g)) ∧
    (∀ f : P → ℝ, Measurable f → (∃ C, ∀ x, |f x| ≤ C) →
      (∀ c : ℝ, M (c • f) = c * M f) ∧ ((∀ x, 0 ≤ f x) → 0 ≤ M f) ∧
      ∀ g : P, M (fun x => f (g * x)) = M f) ∧
    M 1 = 1

end ConnesFeldmanWeiss


